<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 15px; text-align: center; background-color: #e0f2f7; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # 1 – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; font-size: 1.1em; margin-top: 5px;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 4th Grade (CEFR: A1.2)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Visiting the Panama Canal</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> The Vessels Are Big Because...</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: communication: Assist classmates in interpreting homework assignments. concept: Compare a simple classroom object to something familiar.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can assist classmates with Canal-related homework by pointing to relevant visuals (e.g., pointing to the Panama Canal on the map and saying, 'This is the Canal.'). Can compare the Panama Canal to a familiar object using gestures (e.g., 'The Canal is like a big water road.' and 'draws a line.'). Can facilitate peer communication by mimicking (e.g., pretends to steer a vessel for 'vessel in the Panama Canal.').</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333; background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays various pictures of the Panama Canal, including vessels, locks, and the surrounding ocean. Students are asked to identify objects they see and share what they remember about the Canal. Teacher asks, "What do you see?" and "Are the vessels big or small?" This connects to the theme "The Vessels Are Big Because..." and activates prior knowledge.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher points to a picture of a large vessel in the Canal and says, "This is a vessel. It is big. The Panama Canal connects two oceans." Teacher uses gestures to show 'big' and 'connects' (drawing a line). The teacher models the pronunciation of /k/ in 'Canal' and /b/ in 'boat/big'. The teacher then compares a classroom object, like a book, to the Canal using a simple comparison and gesture: "This book is small. The Canal is like a big water road (draws a line with hand)."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is the Canal small or big?" (Checks understanding of 'big/small' and 'canal')</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What does the Panama Canal connect? Does it connect two rivers or two oceans?" (Checks understanding of 'connect' and 'ocean')</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Visiting the Panama Canal"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher presents a simplified "Canal Fact Sheet" with large print and visuals, designed for the "Canal Descriptive Poster" project. This sheet includes simple affirmative and interrogative sentences using present simple, present continuous, and comparatives. For example:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"The Panama Canal is an important waterway." (fact)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"It connects the Atlantic and Pacific Oceans." (fact, discourse marker)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Many vessels travel through the locks every day." (present simple, vocabulary)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Look! Two ships are crossing the Canal now." (present continuous, describing current events)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"That vessel is bigger than this yacht." (comparative)</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Describing facts (e.g., 'The Panama Canal connects two oceans.') and Comparing things (e.g., 'That black vessel is bigger than the white ship.').</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist questions:</b> "What is this paper about?" "What is important about the Canal?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> "The Canal connects two rivers. True or False?" (Teacher guides students to correct false statements).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching:</b> Students match pictures of a vessel/yacht to sentences describing their size (e.g., "The ship is big." matches a picture of a large ship).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10-12 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Mediation</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice using the present simple, present continuous, and comparatives/superlatives to describe and compare, preparing them to assist peers. Focus on vocabulary: canal, boat, ocean, vessel, ship, yacht, big, small, connect, travel.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled practice (Sentence Completion for Poster Facts):</b> The teacher provides sentences related to the "Canal Descriptive Poster" with missing key words. Students complete them individually. Example: "The Panama Canal ____ two ____." (connects, oceans). "Many ____ are ____ through the locks." (vessels, traveling). "That ____ is ____ than this yacht." (ship, bigger). This activity reinforces the spelling of two-syllable words like 'canal' and 'vessel'.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled (Picture Description and Comparison):</b> In pairs, students are given picture cards of different vessels (ship, boat, yacht) and the Canal locks. Student A describes one picture, and Student B asks a question. Then they swap. Example: Student A: "This is a big ship." Student B: "Is it bigger than a yacht?" Student A: "Yes, it is bigger than a yacht." Teacher prompts students to use gestures for 'big', 'small', 'connect'.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction (Homework Interpretation Practice):</b> Students work in pairs. The teacher gives each pair a simple instruction for their poster project (e.g., "Draw a big vessel in the Canal," or "Write a fact about what the Canal connects"). One student (the "explainer") explains the instruction to their partner (the "listener") using simple language and gestures, and points to relevant words or pictures from the fact sheet. The listener confirms understanding. This directly practices assisting in interpreting homework assignments. Sociolinguistic aspect: "Do you understand?" "Yes, I understand."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15-18 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Visiting the Panama Canal"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format: Peer-to-Peer Canal Poster Explanation and Assistance.</b> Students work in small groups of 3-4. Each group has a large piece of paper or whiteboard to start sketching elements for their "Canal Descriptive Poster." One student acts as the "Project Explainer" and is given a specific instruction for the poster (e.g., "Add a fact about what the Canal connects," or "Draw two vessels and show one is bigger than the other."). The "Project Explainer" must clearly explain this instruction to their group members, using words, gestures (e.g., 'draws a line' for 'connects', 'pretends to steer' for 'vessel in the Canal'), and pointing to reference materials (like the Canal Fact Sheet or maps). The group members assist each other to draw or write the element on the poster, asking clarifying questions. This actively develops the 21st Century Skills Project by mediating its creation.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Theme and Scenario:</b> Students are focused on detailing facts about the Panama Canal, specifically how "The Vessels Are Big Because..." they need to travel through this important waterway. They use comparatives to highlight vessel sizes and present simple/continuous to describe the Canal's functions and current activities.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency in explaining, using gestures, simple comparisons ("This is bigger than that"), and polite language (sociolinguistic: 'What do you think?', 'Can I help?') to facilitate communication and collaborative poster creation. Teacher observes and provides scaffolding as needed.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10-12 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task: Paired Mediation Role-Play.</b> Students work in pairs. Each pair is given a card with a specific detail or instruction for their "Canal Descriptive Poster" that a peer might need help with (e.g., "Explain what 'locks' are," "Compare a ship and a yacht," or "Show where the Panama Canal is on a map and say 'This is the Canal.'"). Student A "assists" Student B by explaining the card's content, pointing to visuals, and using gestures. Student B confirms understanding. The teacher uses an observation checklist to assess students' mediation skills.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation of /k/ and /b/ words (Canal, boat, big).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of target vocabulary (canal, vessel, connect, ocean, locks, big, small).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity of explanation (Can the partner understand the instruction/concept?).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Effective use of gestures and pointing to visuals to clarify.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Engaging in respectful conversations (e.g., 'What do you think?', 'Do you like the canal?').</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a quick exit ticket:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Did I help my friend understand something about the Panama Canal today?" (Yes / A little / No)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can I explain why vessels are big?" (Yes / A little / No)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">7-8 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was easy when you tried to help your friend understand about the Canal or the poster?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was difficult, and how can you be a better helper next time?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What specific mediation strategies worked well (e.g., gestures, pointing, simple comparisons)?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Which students effectively used the target grammar (present simple, continuous, comparatives) and vocabulary to explain and assist?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for students who struggled to mediate or interpret information for the Canal Descriptive Poster? (e.g., more visual aids, simpler instructions).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson concludes the unit on "Visiting the Panama Canal" and the "Canal Descriptive Poster" project. The next lesson will likely move to a new theme, but the mediation skills practiced will be useful for future collaborative projects and explanations.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>